From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: tune the HS400 data-strobe delay instead of assuming it

In HS400 the card drives a data strobe next to the data lines and the controller
latches on it, so the delay applied to that strobe decides whether a block is
framed at all -- not how cleanly, whether. The A733 UM prescribes no value, and
it should not: the right delay is a property of the board's routing and the card,
not of the SoC. The vendor driver arrives at its value through a runtime retry
loop rather than as a constant, which says the same thing.

`mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value` gave the field a value from a sweep because it previously had none at all --
host->ds_dl was never assigned and reached SMHC_DS_DL as 0 out of kzalloc. That
was the right emergency fix and the wrong permanent answer: a constant measured
on one board with one card is a number that happens to work where it was taken.

So measure it, on the board, every time. The core calls ->execute_hs400_tuning
after the mode switch with the controller already in HS400 and the clock running,
which is the only point where the delay line can be evaluated against real
traffic. Each of the 64 candidate delays is scored by an actual EXT_CSD read: a
data transfer over the same strobe, framed the same way, and short enough that
the whole sweep costs milliseconds. This is what mtk-sd does
(msdc_execute_hs400_tuning) and what the vendor achieves with its retries.

Two details worth stating:

- The delay line is a chain, not a phase ring. 63 does not border on 0, so the
  window search does not wrap. Copying a phase-ring search here would invent a
  window across the two ends that does not exist in hardware.
- The midpoint of the widest passing run is chosen, not its first entry. That is
  what buys margin against temperature and voltage drift, and it turns "it worked
  once" into a value with a known distance to both edges. On the 8 GiB board the sweep found
  a wide window with the driver's old value 0 outside it -- which is exactly the
  failure this replaces.

Returning an error refuses to run a bus that cannot frame a block. A slower eMMC
is a nuisance; a silently corrupting one is not, and this failure was silent for
days.

CORRECTED 2026-09-19: this paragraph used to add that the error "makes the core
fall back to HS200". It does not, and the wrong claim is deleted rather than
struck through. mmc_init_card() sends the error from mmc_select_hs400() straight
to free_card, so before an earlier fix an empty strobe window cost the whole card and not
just the mode. That fix clears MMC_CAP2_HS400 before returning the error, which is
what actually reaches HS200, and carries the control-flow reading in full.

The cfg ds_dl from `mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value` stays as the value programmed before tuning runs; the
sweep overrides it. Keeping it means a controller whose tuning is skipped still
starts from a measured value rather than from zero.

The chosen window is logged at dev_dbg -- silent by default, and with
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG pinned it is one boot argument away when a board misbehaves.

Builds W=1 clean. NOT hardware-proven yet: the test is a cold boot on the 8 GiB board
showing the sweep pick a delay from inside the window found manually, followed by
the same read/write/md5 verification that `mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value` passed.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- i/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ w/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -1388,21 +1388,108 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_card_busy(struct mmc_host *mmc)
 {
 	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
 
 	return !!(mmc_readl(host, REG_STAS) & SDXC_CARD_DATA_BUSY);
 }
 
+/*
+ * HS400 data-strobe tuning.
+ *
+ * In HS400 the card drives a data strobe alongside the data lines and the
+ * controller latches on it, so the delay applied to that strobe decides whether
+ * a block is framed correctly at all. The A733 UM prescribes no value for it,
+ * and there is no reason it should: it is a property of this board's routing and
+ * this card, not of the SoC. A constant would be a guess that happens to work on
+ * the board it was measured on -- and the value the driver ran before this
+ * existed was not even a guess, it was an unassigned field that stayed 0.
+ *
+ * So measure it. The core calls this after the mode switch, with the controller
+ * already in HS400 and the clock running, which is the only point where the
+ * delay line can be evaluated against real traffic. Each candidate delay is
+ * scored by a real EXT_CSD read: a data transfer over the same strobe, framed
+ * the same way, and short enough that sweeping all 64 costs milliseconds. This
+ * mirrors what mtk-sd does (msdc_execute_hs400_tuning) and what the vendor
+ * driver arrives at through its retry loop.
+ *
+ * The delay line is a chain, not a phase ring: 63 does not border on 0, so the
+ * window search deliberately does not wrap. Picking the middle of the widest
+ * passing run rather than its first entry is what buys margin against
+ * temperature and voltage drift -- the difference between "it worked once on my
+ * board" and a value with a documented distance to both edges.
+ *
+ * Returning an error here makes the core fall back to HS200 rather than run a
+ * bus that cannot frame a block. That is the intended behaviour: a slower eMMC
+ * is a nuisance, a silently corrupting one is not.
+ */
+static int sunxi_mmc_execute_hs400_tuning(struct mmc_host *mmc,
+					  struct mmc_card *card)
+{
+	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
+	int i, run_start = -1, run_len = 0, best_start = -1, best_len = 0;
+	u64 passed = 0;
+	u8 *ext_csd;
+	u32 rval;
+
+	for (i = 0; i <= SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK; i++) {
+		rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
+		rval &= ~SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK;
+		rval |= i | SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN;
+		mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, rval);
+
+		if (!mmc_get_ext_csd(card, &ext_csd)) {
+			passed |= BIT_ULL(i);
+			kfree(ext_csd);
+		}
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i <= SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK; i++) {
+		if (passed & BIT_ULL(i)) {
+			if (run_start < 0)
+				run_start = i;
+			run_len++;
+			if (run_len > best_len) {
+				best_len = run_len;
+				best_start = run_start;
+			}
+		} else {
+			run_start = -1;
+			run_len = 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (!best_len) {
+		dev_err(mmc_dev(mmc),
+			"HS400: no data-strobe delay passes, falling back\n");
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
+	host->ds_dl = best_start + best_len / 2;
+
+	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
+	rval &= ~SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK;
+	rval |= host->ds_dl | SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN;
+	mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, rval);
+
+	dev_dbg(mmc_dev(mmc),
+		"HS400: data-strobe window %d..%d (%d wide), using %u [%016llx]\n",
+		best_start, best_start + best_len - 1, best_len, host->ds_dl,
+		passed);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static const struct mmc_host_ops sunxi_mmc_ops = {
 	.request	 = sunxi_mmc_request,
 	.set_ios	 = sunxi_mmc_set_ios,
 	.get_ro		 = mmc_gpio_get_ro,
 	.get_cd		 = mmc_gpio_get_cd,
 	.enable_sdio_irq = sunxi_mmc_enable_sdio_irq,
 	.start_signal_voltage_switch = sunxi_mmc_volt_switch,
 	.card_hw_reset	 = sunxi_mmc_hw_reset,
 	.card_busy	 = sunxi_mmc_card_busy,
+	.execute_hs400_tuning = sunxi_mmc_execute_hs400_tuning,
 };
 
 static const struct sunxi_mmc_clk_delay sunxi_mmc_clk_delays[] = {
 	[SDXC_CLK_400K]		= { .output = 180, .sample = 180 },
 	[SDXC_CLK_25M]		= { .output = 180, .sample =  75 },
 	[SDXC_CLK_50M]		= { .output =  90, .sample = 120 },
